  "hypothesis": "Aim restoration (DECISIONS #173, owner-directed). The one-bend grammar's flag conflated 'one bend per shot' with 'no initial aim offset', so since the class arms began no mobile gun could launch at 45 degrees off facing and a diagonally-adjacent enemy was unhittable -- never a design ruling; the owner spotted it in watched games. The aim arm restores the +/-1-sector launch offset on every class's mobile gun (specials untouched) on top of the tuned game (rig/wane), measured across all six pairs since the grammar touches every class. Registered prediction: diagonal aim is flank grammar and pulls the over-band bulwark-vs-striker edge (+0.64 pooled) DOWN. SELECTION RULE, flipped by the owner's entertainment ruling (skills and depth are in the game; measurement tunes them): ADOPT the aim arm unless it (a) pushes a fabricator cross-class edge outside |payoff| <= 0.40 by more than 0.10, (b) moves bulwark-vs-striker AWAY from zero, or (c) raises any mirror's draw or capped share by more than 0.05 over its control. T1 anchors: each control cell replicates its latest pooled measurement within 0.25 (bvf -0.383, fvs +0.383, bvs +0.64 band-violation disclosed). Population disclosure: wave-4 doctrine predates the offset grammar, so measured diagonal usage is opportunistic (scaffold-driven bots read the program bounds from the contract; authors hard-coding straight programs simply keep playing straight) -- the aim arm's measured effect is therefore a lower bound, and wave-5 authors doctrine it properly.",
